{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/1119","id":4810247794,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTfIvP88AAAABHraOcg","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/1119","diff_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/1119.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/1119.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/1119","number":1119,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"30004127: accepted negative graph-testing complexity comparison","user":{"login":"AlecKriebel","id":6109370,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMDkzNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6109370?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Accepted full negative answer and published preprint\n\nThe complexity-comparison question of Gishboliner and Shapira (OWR19/2019, p1139, Problem 5; later Problem 5.6) has a negative answer. The single fixed hereditary, extendable property consisting of K4-free graphs that become 3-colorable after deletion of at most one vertex admits a polynomial classical canonical tester on every nonempty finite input size. Every adaptive two-sided vertex-distribution-free tester has superpolynomial worst-case total sample-plus-adjacency cost, even with the vertex set, its size, the full positive rational distribution and the heavy vertex supplied. Distances permit arbitrary edge additions and deletions. This concerns total oracle cost, rather than sample cost with unlimited free queries to arbitrary named vertices; it makes no polynomial running-time claim.\n\nPublished preprint: **A polynomial versus superpolynomial gap in vertex-distribution-free graph testing**, Alec Kriebel, v1.0.0 (2026-10-17), [DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23441523](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23441523). The record contains the five-page note and source/verification archive. The short proof gives an exact heavy-apex distance identity and an elementary factor-36 tripartite-cover reduction from the credited Alon–Shapira (2004) two-sided triangle-testing lower bound. The original self-contained explicit lower-bound proof remains in the verification archive, alongside six programs and their expected results.\n\nRoot review and independent adversaries checked the all-size classical upper bound, arbitrary-repair distance identity, extremal construction, adaptive hidden-label coupling, and the new reduction and source-model bridge. All six public verification programs passed in ordinary and optimized modes; the parent also checked Python 3.12 and 3.14 compatibility. Two NEW successive independent whole-package reviewers, each with no inherited conversation history, passed the identical frozen release with no required repairs. They independently checked both proof routes, primary literature and versions, oracle cost, archive contents and byte-identical portable rebuilding, metadata, and all five PDF pages. [Frozen acceptance and review evidence](https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/tree/bf68897c83271fdefab61610cf19b046e3046078/draft_pr_actual_claims_1100_1200_20261017/audits/pr1119_30004127).\n\nThe extensive priority audit found no prior full answer or identified published full implication for this exact property and strong quantitative model. Classical triangle hardness, parity constructions and other antecedents are credited. This is a bounded audit, not a novelty certificate: the final GS journal body was unavailable and forward-citation retrieval was incomplete. Those limitations are explicit in the paper and metadata.\n\nZenodo readback verified the reviewed files and metadata, with only the reported apostrophe-format normalization in the description; the DOI resolves. The Google Workspace tracker entry at **Math Puzzles!A155:D155** was appended once and read back exactly. The PDF SHA-256 is `a9d81e3b765adc8ee9bee044d23008515e31bbeb57a916358f32e8d3c021a1f0`; the source/verification ZIP SHA-256 is `4367fad39640b11fb1809c2302a91947a9343dd8179590a51ef87e5756caa5cb`.\n\nAI tools were used extensively in development, verification, literature research and writing. The preprint is **unrefereed**; AI adversarial review is not external human peer review, journal acceptance or formal proof-assistant certification.\n\nAccepted source head: `786fff9ba06951209e8c1e3bc6114efbfa2c9d16`. Merge only this exact reviewed PR head.\n","created_at":"2026-10-10T09:37:10Z","updated_at":"2026-10-17T20:06:42Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"a30684c7977af7c0a99b5f944231ccc56cc51e0e","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":true,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/1119/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/1119/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/1119/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/statuses/786fff9ba06951209e8c1e3bc6114efbfa2c9d16","head":{"label":"AlecKriebel:publication/30004127-vdf-negative-comparison","ref":"publication/30004127-vdf-negative-comparison","sha":"786fff9ba06951209